            <ArticleTitle>Minireview: Glucagon in stress and energy homeostasis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1049-54</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1210/en.2011-1979</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Glucagon is traditionally thought of as an antihypoglycemic hormone, for example in response to starvation. However, it actually increases energy expenditure and has other actions not in line with protection from hypoglycemia. Furthermore, it is often found to be elevated when glucose is also raised, for example in circumstances of psychological and metabolic stress. These findings seem more in keeping with glucagon having some role as a hormone enhancing the response to stress.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
